  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Sunday)...\n\nTemperatures will be about 5 to 15 degrees cooler for this \nafternoon compared to yesterdays record setting heat, thanks to a \nweak front that slid through the region overnight. Readings in the\nthe upper 90s to around 100 degrees will still be common, and our\nExtreme Heat Warning continues for much of the area today. In the\nmeantime, monsoonal flow is picking up over the inter-mountain \nwest, tracking into southwestern Montana. We may see a few high- \nbased convective cells over our western zones (Beartooths and \nnearby locations) late today into the early evening. These may \nproduce strong local wind gusts with spotty rainfall. \n\nThe ridge aloft will shift slowly east the next few days. So  \nconditions trend a little cooler from west to east heading into \nmid week, but our eastern areas will continue to see hot \ntemperatures. Very warm overnight lows and high temperatures into \nthe 100 to 105 degree range continue in far eastern Montana and \nSheridan County, Wy even into Wednesday. The Extreme Heat Warning\ncontinues into Tuesday for areas across southeast Montana, \nincluding Billings, and then extends into Wednesday for the \nsoutheast corner of the state due to the prolonged hot daytime \ntemperatures and warm overnight lows.\n\nIn the meantime, mother nature will be switching gears on us from\nthe dry heat we saw Sunday, to a hot humid air mass. Monsoonal \nflow will bring mid level moisture into our western CWA for \nmidweek. At the same time we have easterly low level flow \ntransporting unusually high moisture into our lower elevations. \nThe result is PWATs over 200% of normal across our area at \nmidweek. Dewpoints in the upper 50s and lower 60s may be common \nwith a relatively humid air mass and temps in the lower to mid \n90s. Models suggest weak impulses in the monsoonal southwest flow \ntracking over our western zones. Scattered thunderstorms are \nexpected and a few may produce very heavy rainfall locally. We \nmay need to monitor storms closely for flash flood potential and \nheavy rain impacts. Currently, portions of central Montana are \nmentioned in the excessive rainfall outlook (Weather Prediction \nCenter) this week starting tomorrow. BT",
